/*
:name: concat_array_lane
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: concat_array_lane.top
:pyrope_top: top
:set: compile.formal.mode=normal
*/

// A positional ARRAY spliced into a bit packing, at RUNTIME.
//
// THE RULE (docs/pyrope/10-internals.md, "Bit selection and packing"):
// `X#[..]` is the full bit vector of `X`, and a positional tuple/array packs
// with ENTRY 0 AT BIT 0, each later entry stacked above it. An array is
// ordered by construction -- its entries are numbered, not named -- so unlike
// a multi-field named bundle (rejected, see ../errors/concat_named_bundle_lane.prp)
// it needs no per-field spelling: `lanes#[..]` IS `(lanes[0], lanes[1], lanes[2])#[..]`.
//
// ../pyrope/concat_ordered_array_lane.prp already pins that rule for a
// COMPTIME array, where upass.constprop folds the whole packing away. This
// fixture is the RUNTIME twin, and it covers BOTH array storage classes,
// which reach upass.tolg as two different things:
//
//   * `reg stages:[4]u8`  -- one Memory cell. The splice packs the cell's
//     whole-array `read_all` bus.
//   * `mut lanes:[3]u8`   -- an Array_scalar_view: a single packed comb bus.
//
// In BOTH the packed bus already holds entry 0 in the LOW element window, and
// entry 0 is also the LOW end of the packed word, so the splice is a COPY of
// that bus rather than a per-entry reversal of it. That is the part worth a
// golden: reading the array whole (`x = lanes`) and packing it (`lanes#[..]`)
// agree, which is exactly what changed when `concat` was retired.
//
// `ordering="old"` is required, not decoration: a whole-array read of a
// memory that also carries a same-cycle collision matrix is refused by
// inou.cgen ("no forwarding model" for the inline reg-array emission). It
// also makes `stages[k] = stages[k-1]` an ordinary shift register -- every
// read sees the committed contents -- which is what the golden spells.
//
// The array is left UNINITIALIZED so the design is the packing and nothing
// else: an initializer would add the reset-sweep FSM (a counter, a restore
// write port) and the golden would have to model hardware this claim is not
// about.
//
// Three statements of ONE layout, which must all agree:
//   1. `(inp, ...stages)#[..]`      -- here;
//   2. the hand-written Verilog     -- concat_array_lane.v (prp-equiv-*);
//   3. the explicit per-range form  -- written out twice: as the `assert`s
//      below, so `pass.formal` has to discharge it inside this same file, and
//      as concat_array_lane_1.prp (prp-lec-*), which spells the same ranges
//      with a `for` loop over the entries.
//
// `rw`/`cw` exist only for those asserts; once proven they are dead and
// swept, so the netlist that reaches the LEC is the pure packing one.
pub mod top(inp:u8, p:u8, q:u8) -> (regw:u40@[0], combw:u24@[0]) {
  reg stages:[4]u8:[ordering="old"]
  stages[0] = inp
  stages[1] = stages[0]
  stages[2] = stages[1]
  stages[3] = stages[2]

  mut lanes:[3]u8 = 0
  lanes[0] = inp
  lanes[1] = p
  lanes[2] = q

  // `inp` occupies bits [7..0] and `stages` the 32 above it (entry 0 lowest) --
  // a mixed entry list, so the array splice has to interleave correctly with an
  // ordinary scalar entry rather than only work on its own.
  regw  = (inp, ...stages)#[..]
  combw = lanes#[..]

  // The same layout written one range at a time. Every bit is driven exactly
  // once and no packing appears, so this is an independent statement of where
  // each entry lands.
  mut rw:u40 = 0
  rw#[0..=7]   = inp
  rw#[8..=15]  = stages[0]
  rw#[16..=23] = stages[1]
  rw#[24..=31] = stages[2]
  rw#[32..=39] = stages[3]
  mut cw:u24 = 0
  cw#[0..=7]   = lanes[0]
  cw#[8..=15]  = lanes[1]
  cw#[16..=23] = lanes[2]
  assert(regw == rw, "reg-array splice: entry 0 is the least significant window")
  assert(combw == cw, "comb-array splice: entry 0 is the least significant window")
}
